# ===========================================================================
# flags/ffmpeg.linux.arm64.flags -- Linux aarch64 DELTA over ffmpeg.linux.flags.
#
# WHY THIS TARGET EXISTS AT ALL
#   D:\Nelux\tools\ffmpeg.lock:113-119 defines LINUXARM64_BTBN_* and
#   Nelux/tools/download_ffmpeg.sh consumes it, so nelux ships Linux/aarch64
#   today. Without this file that is the one platform where the "both
#   consumers run the same build" guarantee does not hold, and nelux has to
#   keep a BtbN fallback that WILL 404 when BtbN prunes the autobuild tag.
#
# HOW IT LOADS
#   scripts/lib/flags.sh concatenates ffmpeg.flags, then ffmpeg.linux.flags,
#   then this file. FFmpeg's configure is LAST-WINS, so the three disables
#   below override the enables in ffmpeg.linux.flags rather than duplicating
#   the whole file. Read this as "linux, minus the x86-only vendor SDKs".
#
# BUILD ENVIRONMENT
#   quay.io/pypa/manylinux_2_28_aarch64 (MANYLINUX_IMAGE_AARCH64 in
#   versions.lock) -- AlmaLinux 8 aarch64, glibc 2.28, devtoolset gcc. Same
#   reasoning as x86_64: nelux publishes manylinux_2_28 wheels and auditwheel
#   rejects anything referencing a newer glibc.
#   nasm/yasm are NOT installed for this arch and are not needed -- x264,
#   x265, dav1d, libaom, SVT-AV1 and libvpx all use .S/gas assembly on
#   aarch64, never nasm. docker/Dockerfile.manylinux_2_28 makes that
#   conditional on TARGETARCH.
# ===========================================================================

# --- Intel QSV: does not exist on aarch64 ----------------------------------
# There is no Intel GPU behind an aarch64 CPU in any shipping configuration,
# and the oneVPL dispatcher has no aarch64 runtime. libvpl would also be a
# hard configure failure here: configure:7309-7317 does
#     check_pkg_config libmfx "vpl >= 2.6" ... || die "ERROR: libvpl >= 2.6 not found"
# with no fallback, so leaving it on would abort the build, not degrade it.
--disable-libvpl                              # both -- no aarch64 QSV runtime

# VAAPI is enabled on linux/x86_64 for exactly one reason: the QSV hwcontext
# creates a VAAPI child device. With QSV gone the reason is gone, and turning
# it off keeps libva-devel out of the aarch64 image and libva.so.2 out of the
# shipped dependency closure. Neither consumer ever creates
# AV_HWDEVICE_TYPE_VAAPI (0 hits in Nelux/src; TAS passes no -hwaccel).
--disable-vaapi                               # both -- only ever present for QSV

# --- AMD AMF: no aarch64 runtime -------------------------------------------
# amdgpu-pro / the Mesa AMF bridge are x86_64-only. TAS's h264_amf/hevc_amf
# presets (encodingSettings.py:241-263) simply cannot run on this platform;
# building the encoders would advertise something that always fails at
# runtime with "Cannot load AMF runtime".
--disable-amf                                 # TAS -- no aarch64 AMF runtime

# --- DELIBERATELY KEPT ON ---------------------------------------------------
# ffnvcodec / cuda / nvenc / nvdec / cuvid stay enabled. NVIDIA does ship
# aarch64 Linux drivers (Jetson, Grace-Hopper, ARM servers) and the
# nv-codec-headers are pure stub headers that dlopen libnvcuda.so /
# libnvidia-encode.so at runtime -- there is no aarch64-specific build
# requirement and no cost when the driver is absent.
# gnutls likewise stays on: the yt-dlp FFmpegFD exposure documented in
# flags/ffmpeg.linux.flags is arch-independent.
# libvmaf, libopenh264, libzimg, x264, x265, dav1d, SVT-AV1, libaom, libvpx,
# opus and zlib all build on aarch64 and all stay.
